hey what's up guys my name is echono  welcome back to devlog monday  it's thursday so for those of you not  know where this is a little series which  is essentially just a development log of  hazel my game engine  i am making a game engine which is uh  like i'm not gonna lie some pretty poor  life decisions  led to this moment but i am doing it and  i don't know what else to do so here no  i'm getting jokes aside i really do like  working on this stuff and i have some  pretty exciting stuff to show you today  that is mostly thanks to the community  now a few months ago a man by the name  of karim  messaged me and said that he had to do  an internship as part of his university  studies  and he was wondering if he could do it  for hazel  with me and i was like absolutely now  karim is very interested in graphics and  actually one of the things that he kind  of presented me with which was his  product for university and also kind of  like as a pitch  was essentially a version of hazel that  he had kind of  made into his own game engine which had  a lot more rendering features he had  basically used it like as his final  university project  now this engine had like deferred  rendering it had like  all these various effects like screen  space ambient occlusion a bit of screen  space reflections you know all of this  stuff so it was a lot more fancy  than kind of the rendering features that  were available in hazel at the time and  so karim joined the hazel core team  for three months and i'm about to show  you what he's been working on he's been  primarily working  as a kind of rendering engineer so since  karim joined us as someone working on  rendering  he asked me what i wanted him to do and  i mean there's so many rendering things  that i want to do  but i knew that the first thing that we  need to really be able to handle is  lights i mean that makes sense right at  the moment in hazel we've just been  using like forward rendering  based basic old classic forward  rendering which basically just means  that you have a piece of geometry  you render it and right then and there  you just shade every pixel and you do  every lighting calculation now most  engines  these days use something called deferred  rendering deferred rendering basically  just means that instead of doing that  actual lighting calculation right there  in 3d space with all of your geometry as  you render it for the first time  you basically don't do that instead what  you do is during those kind of 3d  geometry passes you just simply gather  information about the geometry such as  like what is it what materials is it  using where is it in the world like  what are the normals where is it facing  all of that you gather that into a bunch  of textures and then you just do your  actual shading  kind of in screen space on just a quad  that way  you don't have to waste time like  calculating all this different shading  for different things where lights might  not be  in that specific area or maybe it's been  like overwritten by another piece of  geometry there's a lot of benefits  however because you are kind of faking  this like i like to call deferred  rendering fake rendering because  you're not actually rendering stuff in  3d space i mean you are to generate  those buffers in the first place with  all the data that you need  but at the end of the day you're just  actually doing your shading on a  quad and because that's happening it  introduces a lot of issues a lot of them  do have fixes but  i guess conceptually for me it's far  from  ideal so what i told karim to do instead  was to look into forward plus rendering  so i'm not going to explain four plus  rendering in this video i mean you'll  see some of it in action  but forward plus rendering is a little  bit different it's basically  forward rendering that's kind of why  it's called ford plus by the way it has  a lot of names like  clustered forward shading tiled forward  shading  there's a lot like volume tiled forward  shading there's a lot of different names  for it  but essentially what it does is it  reduces  the amount of pixels that have to  process certain lights  thereby optimizing your lights so  essentially what happens is the screen  gets divided into tiles  and these tiles are not just kind of  like x and y tiles but they're actually  they have depth as well they're  basically these little volumes  kind of like voxels but we won't get  too much into that and before we do any  actual like shading and figuring out  what what color like  these meshes that we're drawing in our  scene are we have this pass where we go  through and we work out  what tiles are affected by what lights  and so what happens is  that data is then used to make it so  that we are not processing an excessive  amount of lights per pixel  if you still have like a thousand point  lights on top of each other  and they're all kind of affecting the  same pixels then of course you will  still be slow  but in a real kind of big 3d world like  a scene this huge scene  you can literally have like thousands of  lights and  it's not really going to affect your  performance that much because for any  given kind of group of pixels that  you're rendering  you're only dealing with the local  lights that are in that specific area  and as i mentioned  this works in 3d space because it like  uses these volumes essentially so what  that means is that  if something is in front of the camera  that's obscuring the vision of  other kind of fragments that are in the  background they're not going to  they're not going to be doing any  lighting calculations anyway this is a  lot easier to actually show  and kind of demonstrate you know  visually so let's jump into  hazel and take a look so it looks pretty  much the same but now what we can do is  we can create a point light  and when we create a point light i added  this icon by the way  like 30 minutes ago on stream twitter tv  slash the turner by the way is my live  stream i  stream like i don't really have a  schedule but every now and then when i'm  working i'll just throw up a stream  and so this is what i was doing today  basically adding these icons and playing  around with a with a  like with a few visualization settings  which you'll see in a minute so  basically we have this point light um we  have a few options here like we can  change the color  for example so i can make it like red we  have an intensity  we have a minimum radius we have a  radius and we like the radius is just  like basically  how big the light is and we have a  falloff which kind of tweaks the  fall-off  uh like the way that it does fall off  it's pretty subtle at the moment we're  still  kind of working out what parameters we  actually want to have but this is  basically the way that it works for now  so if i um kind of reduce this maybe  move this up here you know we can  start to play around with this i can  duplicate it i can set the color to  something like blue now we have like a  nice little  uh kind of pink in the middle as the two  lights kind of intertwine  anyway that's the basic idea and i mean  like there's  you know a lot of stuff you could  obviously do with this um  you can have a lot of these lights i  mean at the moment we've actually  limited it to 1000  per scene but that's not like it doesn't  need to be 1000 it can be  realistically we could as long as  they're not in the same place we could  probably render like a million of these  um so it's pretty nice and obviously if  i like drop into this  world and then you can see everything  running as usual  the audio is playing and this is kind of  our normal scene  so um you know in terms of how it works  point lights themselves  are pretty simple um but the benefit  here obviously as i mentioned is the  fact that this is all kind of  uh you know these lights are culled  according to how much kind of range i  guess they take up  and also where they are in the world  meaning that like when we actually  physically shade these pixels we don't  have to worry about like oh there's a  light next to it because if it's not  affecting it then we're not doing the  lighting calculations we don't really  care about that  we're calling it kind of before the  shader even sees any of this  so i've actually added an option over  here it's a little bit difficult to see  but it's over here it's called show  light complexity and so if i turn that  on what you'll actually see is this  little view so  this shows you the tiles which is pretty  nice to see  because you can actually see the tiles  they're 16 by 16 pixel tiles  um and what this what this mode is  intended for  basically showing you is the complexity  of your lights  for a given pixel that you're actually  rendering so you can see that these  this kind of area over here is just not  lit by any point lines that's why it's  kind of the normal color  but then as we get kind of into a more  complex  area basically meaning that there are  more lights affecting that pixel it  basically it's kind of like a heat map  it gets closer to red  so if i let's actually kind of start  over if i delete all these lights  let's add a point light i'll make it  kind of  the radius kind of small so maybe like  three or so meters  i'll move it over here and yeah it looks  a bit weird when you look at it this way  um obviously without this it kind of  looks like a normal point light  you can see it's kind of affecting like  the cube a little bit but this shows you  what it's really affecting  and then if i duplicate this right then  you can kind of see where the lights  overlap now of course this is kind of  dependent on their radius the smaller  the light the less it overlaps that  makes sense right  um you'll also see a little bit of  overlapping from the underside because  of course it is a point light  um so like there's no kind of like you  know the pixels  they don't know that there's stuff kind  of obstructing them but of course if you  were to move this  up sufficiently high then if you look at  it on the other underside you can see  i'm only actually seeing  that kind of other one the second one  not the first one um  and another good example actually is  that if this light  over here we can't click on them yet  which is a little bit annoying  if this light was like behind this  sphere over here for example  then you can see if the sphere is  actually in the way you know  we're not rendering those that that  light kind of isn't being rendered it's  not being calculated because there's a  sphere obscuring the pixels that are  behind the sphere that are lit  so it's pretty clever in that sense um  so okay so let's talk about the light  complexity so if i basically um  have a bunch of these you can see as i  start as i keep duplicating them  we wind up with spots that are slightly  more complex now at the moment the scale  is a bit  i don't know i just made it up basically  16 li at 16 lights it will be red  so if i just basically keep duplicating  it you can see that eventually we get to  red and red just means that hey man  you've got a lot of lights in the same  place  which basically means you might want to  reduce that because it might cause  performance issues you can see it's  totally fine running over here  no problem at all but um might be  something you think about i haven't not  really done too many performance  benchmarks i know that we can handle  like thousands of lights  uh like at 60fps and that's totally fine  but if they're all in the same spot i'm  not sure what that is like really  but yeah so let's open up something a  little bit more interesting just for fun  like sponsor and then we can try adding  a bunch of point lights  and seeing how they affect the scene  okay so what i've done here is just  added a bunch of kind of multi-colored  lights  uh again if we turn on that light  complexity then you can see  what's going on here um they've got  pretty they've got like four meter  ranges so  you can see they're even hitting the  taurus over here um and then the other  thing is you can actually kind of access  this light complexity during actual  gameplay as well so if we  um drop into the world and we take a  look at you know all these  nice lovely lights we can turn on that  light complexity  and now you can see that you know  because obviously it's going to be  different for the editor when you see  like  everything from above it's important to  actually visualize it what it's like  during  you know actual gameplay and you can see  here that we can uh kind of see what's  going on  with all the lights so yeah pretty cool  i think this visualization mode is  really cool because it lets you actually  see  how it works kind of it helps with um  actually uh understanding how forward  plus rendering works  but then also it's obviously useful for  debugging maybe slow  kind of areas in your scene where the  cost of  kind of calculating those those  fragments those pixels  can be a little bit expensive because  there's just a lot of lights  in the same kind of place anyway i hope  you guys enjoyed this video if you did  please don't forget to hit the like  button below  we're getting all these really cool  tools um you know now we can have point  lights um i haven't added spotlights yet  but that's on my list as well  um so we're getting all these tools and  it's i honestly haven't even had a  chance to  use this much um to actually build  scenes  so i'm really excited to be able to do  that um and going forward again  hopefully i'll be able to show you guys  some more exciting scenes than simply  like this sponsor demo  if you have any questions please leave  them in the comment section below again  a huge shout out to karim of course  because he was the person that did the  majority like 95  of the work to get it into this state  karim's internship is coming to an end  and unfortunately i can't afford to hire  him  full-time so he's asked me to mention  that if anyone has  any work or any leads for him any kind  of job opportunities then please let him  know  i can definitely recommend him i think  he's done some amazing work on hazel  if you would like to support hazel's  development you can go to patreon.com  the churno and you'll receive access to  all of this lovely source code  thank you all for watching have a great  rest of your day and i will see you next  time goodbye  [Music]  you

